\begin{align*} 
a+(1/b) + b+(1/a) &= \frac{3a}2 + \frac{3b}{2} \\
&=\frac{3}{2}[a+b]\\
&=\frac{3p}2
\end{align*}  
And,  
\begin{align*} 
(a+(1/b))(b+(1/a))&=\left(\frac{3a}{2}\right)\left(\frac{3b}{2}\right) \\
&=\frac{9}{4} \cdot 2= \frac{9}{2}
\end{align*}  
Furthermore, we also have $(\frac{3a}{2})^2-\frac{3p}2 \cdot \frac{3a}{2}+\frac{9}{2}=0$, so $\frac{3a}{2}$ and $2$ are the roots of $x^2-\frac{3p}2x+\frac{9}{2}$, and a similar argument applying to $b$ and $\frac{3b}{2}$ shows that $\frac{3b}{2}$ is a root of $x^2-\frac{3p}2x+\frac{9}{2}$ as well. The last step is to get rid of the denominators and we have $q=\boxed{9}$. </think> <answer> 9 </answer>

